Subject: Specifying random-slope models
Message-ID: <P59DMjAVSzo.UqlN6wNlAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/P59DMjAVSzo/m/UqlN6wNlAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'm trying to understand the difference between two
ways of specifying random-slope models in INLA:

First,

y ~ 1 + f(G,X,model="iid")

and second,

y ~ 1 + X + f(G,X,model="iid")

Am I correct that the mean of the fixed-effect coefficient
on X in the second specification PLUS the corresponding
mean random effect there should (approximately) equal
the corresponding random effect in the first specification?

If not, then what is the difference between the two?

Many thanks!

From u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com  Mon Dec 14 04:15:48 2020
From: u3eab71733027a24a at groups.google.com (Helpdesk)
Date: Mon, 14 Dec 2020 04:15:48 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] Specifying random-slope models
Message-ID: <P59DMjAVSzo.rmrzh05lAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <P59DMjAVSzo.UqlN6wNlAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <P59DMjAVSzo.UqlN6wNlAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/P59DMjAVSzo/m/rmrzh05lAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Let u be the normal iid effect with unknown precision,  then the first
model says that

	predictor = intercept + X * u

while the second model says that

	predictor = intercept + beta * X + X * u

where beta is the regression coefficient for X.

